# Citation metadata. GitHub renders this as "Cite this repository" in the sidebar.
# Fill in the two TODO fields (legal name, ORCID) before pointing anyone at it;
# a software citation with an unresolvable author is worth very little.
cff-version: 1.2.0
message: >-
  If you use Agent-Memory in research, please cite it as software using the
  metadata below. If a paper on the framework is published, prefer citing the
  paper (this file will be updated with a `preferred-citation` block).
type: software
title: "Agent-Memory: a compact, policy-scored memory layer for LLM agents"
abstract: >-
  Replaces full-transcript replay with a compact, structured, human-readable
  memory (facts, conclusions, principles, arguments, perspectives, user profile)
  selected by a single scoring policy for both context assembly and retention.
  Ships offline benchmarks for token cost, memory fidelity, context rot and
  sycophancy (FlipFlop / SYCON-style) that run against any OpenAI-compatible model.
authors:
  - family-names: "TODO-LEGAL-SURNAME"
    given-names: "TODO-GIVEN-NAMES"
    alias: "XLORD-oss"
    # orcid: "https://orcid.org/0000-0000-0000-0000"   # TODO: free at orcid.org, takes 2 minutes
version: 0.1.0
date-released: "2026-09-12"
license: MIT
repository-code: "https://github.com/XLORD-oss/Agent-Memory"
keywords:
  - llm-agents
  - memory
  - context-engineering
  - context-rot
  - sycophancy
  - self-anchoring
  - token-cost
